      "line_number": 11,
      "line_end": 11,
      "title": "Delete user route lacks admin check",
      "description": "The DELETE /users/:id route omits the requireAdmin middleware that its sibling admin routes use. Any caller reaching the server can delete any user by id. A non-admin caller can delete every account by stepping through user ids. This causes permanent data loss. Add requireAdmin to the route before the handler.",
      "suggested_change": "admin.delete(\"/users/:id\", requireAdmin, async (req, res) => {",
      "source": null,
      "candidate": null,
      "notes": [],
      "problem": "The DELETE /users/:id route omits the requireAdmin middleware that its sibling admin routes use. Any caller reaching the server can delete any user by id.",
      "consequence": "A non-admin caller can delete every account by stepping through user ids. This causes permanent data loss.",
      "fix": "Add requireAdmin to the route before the handler."
    },
    {
      "origin": "agent",
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "bug",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "file_path": "src/admin.js",
      "line_number": 22,
      "line_end": 25,
      "title": "Purge deletions run as floating promises",
      "description": "The forEach callback is async, so the handler never awaits any removeUser call. The handler sends removed: ids.length before any deletion finishes, even when some deletions fail. removeUser throws for an unknown id, and that rejection goes unhandled because Express never sees it. Node then crashes the server process, and the client gets a wrong removed count. Await Promise.all over ids.map(db.removeUser) before responding. Alternatively, use Promise.allSettled and report the count of fulfilled deletions.",
      "suggested_change": "  const results = await Promise.allSettled(ids.map((id) => db.removeUser(id)));\n  res.json({ removed: results.filter((r) => r.status === \"fulfilled\").length });",
      "source": "lens:async-floating-promise",
      "candidate": null,
      "notes": [],
      "problem": "The forEach callback is async, so the handler never awaits any removeUser call. The handler sends removed: ids.length before any deletion finishes, even when some deletions fail.",
      "consequence": "removeUser throws for an unknown id, and that rejection goes unhandled because Express never sees it. Node then crashes the server process, and the client gets a wrong removed count.",
      "fix": "Await Promise.all over ids.map(db.removeUser) before responding. Alternatively, use Promise.allSettled and report the count of fulfilled deletions."
    },
    {
      "origin": "agent",
      "severity": "minor",
      "category": "maintainability",
      "confidence": 0.75,
      "file_path": "src/admin.js",
